EXTREMAL CORES BY AXIS (2<=|O|<=5) -- do different graders pick DIFFERENT extremes? (=> multi-axis)
 Axis 1 (gap = worst-mode concentration, the certificate):
   most CONCENTRATED (min gap): [0, 1, 2, 5, 6](0.198), [0, 2, 3, 4, 5](0.198), [0, 1, 3, 4, 6](0.198)
   most SPREAD (max gap): [1, 2, 4](2.000), [3, 5, 6](2.000), [0, 1, 2, 4](2.000)
 Axis 2 (flatness = global difference-set-ness):
   most CONCENTRATED (min flat): [0, 2, 4, 6](0.500), [0, 1, 3, 5](0.500), [0, 1, 2, 3](0.500)
   most SPREAD (max flat): [1, 2, 6](1.000), [1, 3, 4](1.000), [2, 4, 5](1.000)
 Axis 2' (defect = distance from difference set):
   most high-defect (concentrated): [0, 1, 2](0.667), [0, 1, 4](0.667), [0, 1, 6](0.667)
   most low-defect (diff-set-like): [0, 1, 3](0.000), [0, 1, 5](0.000), [0, 2, 3](0.000)

 KEY DISAGREEMENT: gap says the DOUBLET is most concentrated (0.198); flatness/defect say the
 ARC {0,1,2,3} is (flat 0.5, defect 0.667). Two DIFFERENT 'most concentrated' cores => 2 axes.
